WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:07.640
Did you know that there are unbelievable research&nbsp;
papers on how simulate the life of virtual trees?&nbsp;

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:14.160
We can write a computer program to simulate&nbsp;
how they move by interacting with them with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.960
mouse. If we go too hard, we can simulate&nbsp;
how they break, and if we feel like it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:28.000
we can even burn a tree down. Or simulate not&nbsp;
one burning tree, but even an entire forest.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.480
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.480
Now, for a more peaceful simulation,&nbsp;
we can simulate the life of complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:44.480
ecosystems for a thousand years as well.&nbsp;
This is a story of human ingenuity. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:50.800
all of these were handcrafted techniques,&nbsp;
no AI of any sort was used to make this.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:57.280
And I have good news for you Fellow Scholars.&nbsp;
We have even more papers in this area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.320
and I have found some new ones that&nbsp;
are beyond amazing. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:09.080
the previous works showed what happens above&nbsp;
the ground, but a great deal of a tree’s life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:15.880
also happens underground. As this poplar&nbsp;
tree develops over time, so do its roots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:22.880
as well. Now take a careful look at the shape&nbsp;
of the roots. And now, have a look at this pine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:32.360
tree growing. Wow. The poplar went vertical, the&nbsp;
pine went horizontal. Now, why does this happen?

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:38.400
Well, poplar trees are rapid-growing&nbsp;
but relatively short-lived trees and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:45.360
they primarily grow in areas where water is&nbsp;
deeper down in the soil, and with these roots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:53.080
they can reach them. Now, the pine sees the world&nbsp;
entirely differently. Its roots are horizontal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:59.560
thus maximizing water and nutrient absorption&nbsp;
near the surface. And as an additional bonus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:06.520
it gets stabilized in unfriendly environments&nbsp;
where the soil is looser. Very smart.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:16.200
Now, what about the oak? The oak a wise tree and&nbsp;
what does a wise tree do? Of course, diversifies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:24.480
its bets. Its deep taproot system gives it access&nbsp;
to deep water sources, but it also has widespread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:32.280
horizontal roots for maximum nutrient absorption,&nbsp;
thus giving it great longevity. Great choice.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.240
Hmm…I feel a bit like David&nbsp;
Attenborough here. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:43.760
the story does not end here. A good tree root&nbsp;
simulation has to take into consideration how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:50.920
smart and adaptable these little plants are.&nbsp;
You see, environmental stimulus matters a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:56.280
deal in their development. For instance,&nbsp;
when growing around the slope of a hill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:01.600
it recognizes that it will need a different&nbsp;
kind of root system to survive. Loving it!

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:09.600
So, is that it? Is that all this simulator can&nbsp;
take into consideration? Well, not quite. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:17.240
hold on to your papers and look at this Fellow&nbsp;
Scholars. Holy mother of papers! It simulates the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:23.240
effect of all of these parameters, and this&nbsp;
is not even a complete list. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:29.960
it simulates how different kinds of soils&nbsp;
exist and show a different growth resistance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:37.120
factor. And we can also mix them up, so it can&nbsp;
simulate heterogeneous systems. What does that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:44.720
mean? Multiple soil types scattered across&nbsp;
this virtual world. Absolutely beautiful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:51.680
And the tree will grow as a tree should grow&nbsp;
in reality. Hopefully. And as we change the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:58.120
distribution of soil types, so does this&nbsp;
wise little tree adapt to its environment.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:03.880
It even learns to avoid regions of&nbsp;
high growth resistance and it grows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:09.000
around it. Thus simulating&nbsp;
asymmetric growth. So good!

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.320
Now, I wonder how much computation&nbsp;
does such a simulation take? I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:21.440
we need to simulate these little virtual&nbsp;
trees for years and years. Does it take years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:28.480
and years of computation time in real life?&nbsp;
Look at that! Not at all. Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:35.600
we can simulate such a system on the scale&nbsp;
of months in a matter of seconds. Simulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:44.080
60k little roots in less than 3 seconds.&nbsp;
And sometimes, even interactively. Wow.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:48.600
Now at this point I have one more&nbsp;
question left. You know what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:55.680
is. Are these simulations for show, or is this&nbsp;
how reality works? Well, let’s see about that.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:01.280
When compared to a previous paper that&nbsp;
digitizes tree root systems in real life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:09.160
this is the real deal. Now, does this simulator&nbsp;
live up to these results? Look at that. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:15.200
goodness! Clean sweep. Loving it. Not only&nbsp;
the kinds of structures are very similar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:22.640
but I actually have to look real close to find&nbsp;
the differences. It needn’t be perfect, and yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:30.080
it almost is. Wow. And it gets better. The&nbsp;
root and shoot ratios for real trees are known,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:36.680
and the simulated oak’s properties are&nbsp;
very similar the ones seen in real trees.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:43.080
And now, imagine that the games and virtual&nbsp;
worlds of the future could be with simulations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:50.200
that are this realistic. We could play in these&nbsp;
worlds in the future. What a time to be alive!

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:54.160
And once again, all this&nbsp;
was done through sheer human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:58.200
ingenuity. No AI technique was used in this paper.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:04.640
And the paper contains so much more analysis, you&nbsp;
simply have to take a look. A beautiful piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:12.800
work. Of course it is, because this is a SIGGRAPH&nbsp;
paper. What is that? SIGGRAPH is the most sacred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:19.000
place for some of the best computer graphics&nbsp;
papers ever. Getting a paper accepted there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:26.600
is in my opinion, like winning the olympic gold&nbsp;
medal of graphics research. Huge congratulations.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:32.840
However, computer graphics is a field that is&nbsp;
orders of magnitude smaller than AI research.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:41.600
For instance, look at this. Goodness. This paper&nbsp;
was seen by about 200 Fellow Scholars. And I fear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:47.920
that if we don’t talk about works like this here&nbsp;
on Two Minute Papers, almost no one will. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:53.760
I try to be a good ambassador of computer&nbsp;
graphics research and show everyone how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:07:00.960
amazing these papers are and they can give to the&nbsp;
world so much. And it breaks my heart that so few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:08.280
people know about them. And good news, I have more&nbsp;
papers like this up the sleeve. If you wish to not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:14.400
miss out on them when they come in the future,&nbsp;
make sure to subscribe and hit the bell icon.

